1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,143
A phone gets a message through a wall.

2
00:00:03,143 --> 00:00:05,139
A microwave oven warms food.

3
00:00:05,139 --> 00:00:06,931
A heater coil glows red.

4
00:00:07,331 --> 00:00:08,772
Three different jobs.

5
00:00:08,772 --> 00:00:10,983
One kind of wave does all three.

6
00:00:11,433 --> 00:00:13,648
Aarav sees it at his study table.

7
00:00:13,648 --> 00:00:16,740
His lamp lights the page,
and his phone buzzes.

8
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:21,291
The light and the phone signal are
the same kind of wave, just different

9
00:00:21,291 --> 00:00:22,191
sizes.

10
00:00:22,349 --> 00:00:26,567
By the end, you will measure a wave,
and find the energy in one packet

11
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:27,467
of light.

12
00:00:27,961 --> 00:00:30,708
So what is electromagnetic radiation?

13
00:00:31,058 --> 00:00:35,753
In eighteen seventy, James Maxwell
showed that an accelerating charge sends

14
00:00:35,753 --> 00:00:40,027
out electric and magnetic fields,
travelling together as a wave.

15
00:00:40,377 --> 00:00:44,329
The two fields stand at right angles
to each other, and to the direction

16
00:00:44,329 --> 00:00:45,345
the wave moves.

17
00:00:45,695 --> 00:00:47,124
They need no medium.

18
00:00:47,124 --> 00:00:49,626
They cross empty space, like sunlight.

19
00:00:49,976 --> 00:00:54,233
And in a vacuum, all of them travel
at one speed, c, three times ten to

20
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:55,940
the eight metres per second.

21
00:00:56,590 --> 00:00:59,058
Let's measure one on our molecule bench.

22
00:00:59,408 --> 00:01:02,976
The distance from one crest to the
next is the wavelength, lambda.

23
00:01:03,376 --> 00:01:06,308
Now count the crests passing
this point each second.

24
00:01:06,308 --> 00:01:09,811
That is the frequency, nu, in
hertz, after Heinrich Hertz.

25
00:01:10,211 --> 00:01:11,888
Squeeze the waves closer.

26
00:01:11,888 --> 00:01:15,927
Shorter wavelength, more crests
each second: a higher frequency.

27
00:01:16,327 --> 00:01:18,058
The speed never changes.

28
00:01:18,058 --> 00:01:21,049
So wavelength and
frequency always trade off.

29
00:01:21,699 --> 00:01:26,516
Line up all these waves by size, and
you get the electromagnetic spectrum.

30
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:32,341
Radio waves are the longest, then
microwaves, infrared, visible, ultraviolet,

31
00:01:32,341 --> 00:01:35,292
X rays, and gamma rays, the shortest.

32
00:01:35,692 --> 00:01:40,972
Our eyes see only a thin slice, from
violet at about four hundred nanometres,

33
00:01:40,972 --> 00:01:43,120
to red at about seven fifty.

34
00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:46,234
Now the formulas, one step at a time.

35
00:01:46,584 --> 00:01:47,484
Step one.

36
00:01:47,381 --> 00:01:50,471
Each second, nu waves
pass, each lambda long.

37
00:01:50,471 --> 00:01:54,562
So the distance covered per
second, c, equals nu times lambda.

38
00:01:55,312 --> 00:01:56,212
Step two.

39
00:01:56,137 --> 00:02:00,534
So frequency is c over lambda,
in hertz, which means per second.

40
00:02:01,284 --> 00:02:02,184
Step three.

41
00:02:02,172 --> 00:02:04,893
Wavenumber, nu bar, is one over lambda.

42
00:02:04,893 --> 00:02:06,679
It counts waves per metre.

43
00:02:07,429 --> 00:02:08,377
Step four.

44
00:02:08,377 --> 00:02:10,504
Always put lambda in metres.

45
00:02:10,504 --> 00:02:15,270
Four hundred nanometres is four
times ten to the minus seven metres.

46
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:17,166
Side by side.

47
00:02:17,166 --> 00:02:22,228
Red light: seven fifty nanometres,
four times ten to the fourteen hertz.

48
00:02:22,228 --> 00:02:27,077
Violet light: four hundred nanometres,
seven point five times ten to the

49
00:02:27,077 --> 00:02:28,266
fourteen hertz.

50
00:02:28,666 --> 00:02:29,822
Pause and think.

51
00:02:29,822 --> 00:02:32,763
Why does violet light
have the higher frequency?

52
00:02:35,963 --> 00:02:37,880
Because its waves are shorter.

53
00:02:37,880 --> 00:02:41,535
The speed is fixed, so shorter
waves must pass more often.

54
00:02:42,185 --> 00:02:44,655
But waves could not explain one thing.

55
00:02:44,655 --> 00:02:49,356
A heated iron rod glows dull red, then
brighter red, then white, then blue

56
00:02:49,356 --> 00:02:50,692
as it gets hotter.

57
00:02:51,092 --> 00:02:54,097
In nineteen hundred,
Max Planck explained it.

58
00:02:54,097 --> 00:02:56,347
Energy is not given out smoothly.

59
00:02:56,347 --> 00:02:59,103
It comes in tiny packets, called quanta.

60
00:02:59,503 --> 00:03:02,775
The energy of one packet is E equals h nu.

61
00:03:02,775 --> 00:03:08,042
h is Planck's constant, six point six
two six times ten to the minus thirty

62
00:03:08,042 --> 00:03:09,572
four joule seconds.

63
00:03:09,972 --> 00:03:14,905
So energy comes as h nu, two h
nu, three h nu, never in between.

64
00:03:14,905 --> 00:03:17,228
Higher frequency, bigger packet.

65
00:03:17,878 --> 00:03:18,959
Remember this.

66
00:03:18,959 --> 00:03:22,859
Light travels as a wave, but
its energy comes in packets.

67
00:03:23,559 --> 00:03:25,295
Now, one worked example.

68
00:03:25,595 --> 00:03:29,294
Green light has a wavelength
of five hundred nanometres.

69
00:03:29,294 --> 00:03:31,469
Find the energy of one photon.

70
00:03:31,969 --> 00:03:33,791
First, lambda in metres.

71
00:03:33,791 --> 00:03:36,621
Five times ten to the minus seven metres.

72
00:03:37,371 --> 00:03:39,271
Frequency is c over lambda.

73
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:44,020
Three times ten to the eight, over
five times ten to the minus seven, is

74
00:03:44,020 --> 00:03:46,490
six times ten to the fourteen hertz.

75
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,968
Energy is h times nu.

76
00:03:48,968 --> 00:03:54,045
Six point six two six times ten to
the minus thirty four, times six times

77
00:03:54,045 --> 00:03:55,654
ten to the fourteen.

78
00:03:56,404 --> 00:04:00,974
Three point nine eight times ten to
the minus nineteen joules, for one

79
00:04:00,974 --> 00:04:01,874
photon.

80
00:04:02,305 --> 00:04:03,771
Now, the exam trap.

81
00:04:04,121 --> 00:04:08,886
Some students put lambda in nanometres
straight into c equals nu lambda.

82
00:04:09,286 --> 00:04:12,818
That makes the frequency
a billion times too small.

83
00:04:12,818 --> 00:04:15,198
Change nanometres to metres first.

84
00:04:15,198 --> 00:04:18,466
One nanometre is ten to
the minus nine metres.

85
00:04:19,116 --> 00:04:20,575
Where is this asked?

86
00:04:20,575 --> 00:04:25,039
As numericals linking wavelength,
frequency and photon energy, and on the

87
00:04:25,039 --> 00:04:26,595
order of the spectrum.

88
00:04:27,195 --> 00:04:28,442
Exam level now.

89
00:04:28,442 --> 00:04:33,778
A sixty watt lamp gives out all its
energy as light of six hundred nanometres.

90
00:04:33,778 --> 00:04:37,043
How many photons does
it give out each second?

91
00:04:37,043 --> 00:04:38,342
Pause and solve.

92
00:04:43,742 --> 00:04:45,052
The answer is D.

93
00:04:45,052 --> 00:04:49,235
One point eight one times ten to
the twenty photons per second.

94
00:04:49,735 --> 00:04:52,547
One photon's energy is h c over lambda.

95
00:04:52,547 --> 00:04:57,336
That is three point three one times
ten to the minus nineteen joules.

96
00:04:57,836 --> 00:05:00,983
Sixty watts means sixty
joules every second.

97
00:05:00,983 --> 00:05:03,571
Divide sixty by one photon's energy.

98
00:05:04,071 --> 00:05:07,180
One point eight one
times ten to the twenty.

99
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:09,207
B kept lambda in nanometres.

100
00:05:09,207 --> 00:05:11,027
A is one photon's energy.

101
00:05:11,627 --> 00:05:13,085
Here is your recap.

102
00:05:13,085 --> 00:05:14,583
Screenshot this card.

103
00:05:17,783 --> 00:05:20,620
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

104
00:05:21,020 --> 00:05:21,920
One.

105
00:05:21,513 --> 00:05:25,338
How are the speed, frequency
and wavelength of light linked?

106
00:05:26,138 --> 00:05:27,038
Two.

107
00:05:26,674 --> 00:05:30,884
Which has more energy per photon,
red light or violet light?

108
00:05:31,684 --> 00:05:32,584
Three.

109
00:05:32,290 --> 00:05:35,617
What did Planck say about
how energy is given out?

110
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:39,991
Next time: can light knock
electrons out of a metal?

111
00:05:39,991 --> 00:05:44,424
We find out in the photoelectric
effect and the dual nature of matter.
